1
00:00:00,120 --> 00:00:03,555
On Wednesday the twenty-first
of October, twenty twenty-six,

2
00:00:03,622 --> 00:00:07,233
two documents about the same
company came out in the same day.

3
00:00:07,300 --> 00:00:08,053
In California,

4
00:00:08,120 --> 00:00:12,683
Meta reported sixty-four point six billion
dollars of revenue in three months.

5
00:00:12,750 --> 00:00:17,210
In Santa Fe, New Mexico, a judge ordered
it to pay twenty-five billion dollars.

6
00:00:17,640 --> 00:00:22,169
This is the story of how one personality
quiz turned into eight years of bills,

7
00:00:22,236 --> 00:00:24,330
and how one state refused to settle.

8
00:00:24,750 --> 00:00:28,143
Everyone says Meta was finally
punished for Cambridge Analytica.

9
00:00:28,210 --> 00:00:30,583
But Meta has not paid
a single dollar of it.

10
00:00:30,650 --> 00:00:34,600
It has appealed. And the case was
never only about Cambridge Analytica.

11
00:00:35,030 --> 00:00:39,387
So how does a company survive the
most famous data scandal of its time,

12
00:00:39,454 --> 00:00:40,943
and keep growing anyway?

13
00:00:41,010 --> 00:00:45,070
Because the scandal was not a bug. It was
the business working exactly as designed.

14
00:00:45,500 --> 00:00:49,500
Keep an eye on the bill. It is the only
thing in this story that never gets paid.

15
00:00:49,920 --> 00:00:53,343
It started at Harvard, on the fourth
of February, two thousand four.

16
00:00:53,410 --> 00:00:56,940
Mark Zuckerberg and Eduardo Saverin
each put in a thousand dollars.

17
00:00:57,370 --> 00:00:57,966
A year later,

18
00:00:58,033 --> 00:01:00,259
the company paid two
hundred thousand dollars

19
00:01:00,326 --> 00:01:02,110
just for the name facebook dot com.

20
00:01:02,540 --> 00:01:06,850
In May twenty twelve, Facebook went public
and raised about sixteen billion dollars.

21
00:01:07,270 --> 00:01:10,202
A month earlier, it had bought
Instagram for about one billion.

22
00:01:10,269 --> 00:01:13,023
Instagram had thirty million
users and thirteen employees.

23
00:01:13,090 --> 00:01:16,889
Two years later, it paid about
nineteen billion dollars for WhatsApp,

24
00:01:16,956 --> 00:01:20,990
which had four hundred and fifty
million users and fifty-five employees.

25
00:01:21,420 --> 00:01:24,803
The users never paid anything. The
advertisers paid for the users.

26
00:01:24,870 --> 00:01:28,170
And the more Facebook knew about each
person, the more each advert was worth.

27
00:01:28,600 --> 00:01:29,302
In those years,

28
00:01:29,369 --> 00:01:32,788
an app on Facebook could read more
than the person who installed it.

29
00:01:32,855 --> 00:01:34,290
It could read their friends.

30
00:01:34,720 --> 00:01:36,146
That same year, twenty twelve,

31
00:01:36,213 --> 00:01:39,133
Facebook had made a promise to
the Federal Trade Commission.

32
00:01:39,200 --> 00:01:41,533
It would not mislead
users about their privacy.

33
00:01:41,600 --> 00:01:44,420
Remember that promise. It
comes back with a price tag.

34
00:01:44,840 --> 00:01:49,107
So how does one personality quiz end
up costing twenty-five billion dollars?

35
00:01:49,174 --> 00:01:51,170
It starts with that one permission.

36
00:01:51,587 --> 00:01:52,579
In twenty fourteen,

37
00:01:52,646 --> 00:01:55,979
a Cambridge University researcher
named Aleksandr Kogan built

38
00:01:56,046 --> 00:01:59,057
a personality quiz called
"This is your digital life."

39
00:01:59,487 --> 00:02:02,180
About two hundred and seventy
thousand people downloaded it.

40
00:02:02,247 --> 00:02:05,240
Through their friend lists, the app
reached far more people than that.

41
00:02:05,307 --> 00:02:06,478
Facebook's own estimate,

42
00:02:06,545 --> 00:02:09,936
published by its chief technology
officer in April twenty eighteen,

43
00:02:10,003 --> 00:02:12,067
was up to eighty-seven million profiles.

44
00:02:12,487 --> 00:02:14,854
Other counts say fifty
million, or sixty-five.

45
00:02:14,921 --> 00:02:17,720
Nobody outside the company
has ever counted them all.

46
00:02:17,787 --> 00:02:21,877
About eighty-two percent of those profiles
belonged to people in the United States.

47
00:02:22,317 --> 00:02:24,191
Kogan passed the data to a political

48
00:02:24,258 --> 00:02:27,100
consulting firm in London
called Cambridge Analytica.

49
00:02:27,167 --> 00:02:29,809
It was bankrolled by the
billionaire Robert Mercer,

50
00:02:29,876 --> 00:02:32,107
and his daughter Rebekah sat on its board.

51
00:02:32,527 --> 00:02:36,987
In twenty sixteen, the Trump campaign
paid it five point nine million dollars,

52
00:02:37,054 --> 00:02:41,110
and the Cruz campaign five point
eight million, election filings show.

53
00:02:41,177 --> 00:02:44,057
Whether any of it changed a
single vote is still disputed.

54
00:02:44,487 --> 00:02:47,460
In twenty fifteen, Facebook found
out where the data had gone.

55
00:02:47,527 --> 00:02:51,860
It asked for the data to be deleted, and
it accepted signed letters saying it was.

56
00:02:51,927 --> 00:02:55,227
It did not check. And it
did not tell the users.

57
00:02:55,657 --> 00:02:58,072
That decision, made
quietly in twenty fifteen,

58
00:02:58,139 --> 00:03:00,297
is the start of every bill in this film.

59
00:03:00,720 --> 00:03:05,097
For three years, nothing happened. Then,
on Friday the sixteenth of March,

60
00:03:05,164 --> 00:03:09,542
twenty eighteen, Facebook suspended
Cambridge Analytica from its platform,

61
00:03:09,609 --> 00:03:10,810
late in the evening.

62
00:03:11,240 --> 00:03:12,042
The next morning,

63
00:03:12,109 --> 00:03:15,833
The Observer and The New York Times
published within hours of each other.

64
00:03:15,900 --> 00:03:19,770
Facebook had suspended Kogan and Wylie
too, but the story was already out.

65
00:03:20,200 --> 00:03:21,911
Their source was Christopher Wylie,

66
00:03:21,978 --> 00:03:24,670
a former director of research
at Cambridge Analytica.

67
00:03:25,100 --> 00:03:25,934
Two days later,

68
00:03:26,001 --> 00:03:31,103
Channel 4 News aired undercover footage of
the firm's chief executive, Alexander Nix.

69
00:03:31,170 --> 00:03:32,841
He talked about bribery stings,

70
00:03:32,908 --> 00:03:35,880
honey traps and front
companies as possible services.

71
00:03:36,310 --> 00:03:38,969
His board suspended him
the next day. The same day,

72
00:03:39,036 --> 00:03:41,214
Britain's data regulator asked a court for

73
00:03:41,281 --> 00:03:43,740
a warrant to search the
firm's London offices.

74
00:03:44,160 --> 00:03:46,343
Mark Zuckerberg said
nothing for five days.

75
00:03:46,410 --> 00:03:49,290
Then, on the twenty-first of
March, he posted a statement.

76
00:03:49,720 --> 00:03:52,533
In April, he sat in front
of the United States Senate,

77
00:03:52,600 --> 00:03:54,673
and the next day in front of the House.

78
00:03:54,740 --> 00:03:59,248
ZUCK: It was my mistake, and I'm
sorry. I started Facebook, I run it,

79
00:03:59,315 --> 00:04:02,100
and I'm responsible for what happens here.

80
00:04:02,530 --> 00:04:06,053
He also confirmed that his own data
had been swept up with everyone else's.

81
00:04:06,120 --> 00:04:08,680
And he promised to grow
Facebook's security staff to

82
00:04:08,747 --> 00:04:11,070
twenty thousand people
by the end of the year.

83
00:04:11,500 --> 00:04:16,013
On the second of May, Cambridge Analytica
announced it was ceasing all operations.

84
00:04:16,080 --> 00:04:18,294
It blamed a "siege of media coverage" that

85
00:04:18,361 --> 00:04:20,750
had driven away almost
all of its customers.

86
00:04:21,180 --> 00:04:23,783
The firm that used the data
was gone in about six weeks.

87
00:04:23,850 --> 00:04:27,970
Two years later, Nix accepted a seven-year
ban on running British companies.

88
00:04:28,400 --> 00:04:31,490
But the company that gave the data
away was just getting started.

89
00:04:31,920 --> 00:04:36,193
So what does a scandal like that
actually cost a company like this?

90
00:04:36,260 --> 00:04:38,633
The first bill came from the stock market.

91
00:04:38,700 --> 00:04:41,201
On the twenty-sixth of
July, twenty eighteen,

92
00:04:41,268 --> 00:04:44,350
Facebook shares fell nineteen
percent in a single day.

93
00:04:44,780 --> 00:04:49,843
About one hundred and nineteen billion
dollars of market value disappeared,

94
00:04:49,910 --> 00:04:53,740
the largest one-day loss for
any US company at the time.

95
00:04:54,170 --> 00:04:58,443
In October, Britain's data regulator
fined it five hundred thousand pounds.

96
00:04:58,510 --> 00:05:02,360
It found that at least one million
British users had been put at risk.

97
00:05:02,790 --> 00:05:04,212
That was the legal maximum.

98
00:05:04,279 --> 00:05:08,800
One tech news site worked out it was about
seventeen minutes of Facebook's profit.

99
00:05:09,230 --> 00:05:10,961
In February twenty nineteen,

100
00:05:11,028 --> 00:05:13,338
a committee of the British Parliament

101
00:05:13,405 --> 00:05:16,673
called companies like
Facebook "digital gangsters."

102
00:05:16,740 --> 00:05:20,760
Zuckerberg had refused its invitations
to give evidence three times.

103
00:05:21,180 --> 00:05:24,100
Then, on the twenty-fourth
of July, twenty nineteen,

104
00:05:24,167 --> 00:05:28,130
the Federal Trade Commission announced
a five billion dollar penalty.

105
00:05:28,560 --> 00:05:31,546
It was the largest privacy
penalty ever imposed at the time.

106
00:05:31,613 --> 00:05:34,920
But it was about nine percent of
Facebook's revenue for one year.

107
00:05:35,350 --> 00:05:38,876
The order also put a new privacy
committee on Facebook's board,

108
00:05:38,943 --> 00:05:42,650
and made Zuckerberg personally
sign off on privacy every quarter.

109
00:05:43,080 --> 00:05:46,945
The same day, the SEC added
one hundred million dollars,

110
00:05:47,012 --> 00:05:51,719
because Facebook had told investors
that user data "may be" misused,

111
00:05:51,786 --> 00:05:53,253
when it already knew.

112
00:05:53,320 --> 00:05:54,970
It had known for about two years.

113
00:05:55,400 --> 00:05:56,662
In twenty twenty-two,

114
00:05:56,729 --> 00:06:00,208
Meta agreed to pay seven
hundred and twenty-five million

115
00:06:00,275 --> 00:06:03,433
dollars to settle a class
action brought by users.

116
00:06:03,500 --> 00:06:07,670
Shared among up to eighty-seven million
people, that was a few dollars each.

117
00:06:08,100 --> 00:06:11,953
In other privacy cases, Texas got
one point four billion dollars.

118
00:06:12,020 --> 00:06:15,323
And Ireland's regulator fined
it one point two billion euros.

119
00:06:15,390 --> 00:06:16,749
In twenty twenty-five,

120
00:06:16,816 --> 00:06:20,703
shareholders who had sued
Zuckerberg and other executives for

121
00:06:20,770 --> 00:06:24,398
eight billion dollars settled
in the middle of the trial,

122
00:06:24,465 --> 00:06:26,150
on terms nobody disclosed.

123
00:06:26,580 --> 00:06:29,850
Every one of these was a record. But
every one of them was survivable.

124
00:06:30,280 --> 00:06:32,683
In twenty twenty-one,
Facebook renamed itself Meta.

125
00:06:32,750 --> 00:06:36,760
By twenty twenty-five, its yearly revenue
had passed two hundred billion dollars.

126
00:06:37,187 --> 00:06:39,120
One state did not settle.

127
00:06:39,187 --> 00:06:40,248
In twenty twenty-one,

128
00:06:40,315 --> 00:06:43,480
New Mexico sued Facebook under
its consumer protection law.

129
00:06:43,547 --> 00:06:46,998
The attorney general who filed it
was Hector Balderas. His successor,

130
00:06:47,065 --> 00:06:48,697
Raul Torrez, took the case over.

131
00:06:49,127 --> 00:06:50,589
In August twenty twenty-six,

132
00:06:50,656 --> 00:06:54,200
a group of other states signed a
settlement with Meta in Oakland.

133
00:06:54,267 --> 00:06:57,147
New Mexico refused to sign it.
It took its case to a jury.

134
00:06:57,577 --> 00:07:01,494
One warning, because the headlines
mix them up. New Mexico has a second,

135
00:07:01,561 --> 00:07:03,874
separate case against
Meta, about children.

136
00:07:03,941 --> 00:07:05,767
This film is about the first one.

137
00:07:06,197 --> 00:07:08,137
But the case was bigger than one quiz app.

138
00:07:08,207 --> 00:07:12,592
It was about twenty-nine public
statements Meta and its leaders had made,

139
00:07:12,659 --> 00:07:16,197
about privacy, hate speech,
misinformation and moderation.

140
00:07:16,627 --> 00:07:20,510
On Friday the twenty-fifth of
September, twenty twenty-six,

141
00:07:20,577 --> 00:07:22,980
a jury in Santa Fe gave its verdict.

142
00:07:23,047 --> 00:07:26,257
Twenty-six of those twenty-nine
statements were misleading.

143
00:07:26,687 --> 00:07:30,837
And then the jury did something no
headline explained well. It counted.

144
00:07:31,253 --> 00:07:34,676
New Mexico's law sets a penalty
for every single violation.

145
00:07:34,743 --> 00:07:39,293
So the question became: how many
violations is one misleading statement?

146
00:07:39,723 --> 00:07:42,351
The answer used in court
was each statement,

147
00:07:42,418 --> 00:07:46,086
multiplied by everyone in New
Mexico who could have seen it.

148
00:07:46,153 --> 00:07:49,043
Not everyone who did see
it. Everyone who could.

149
00:07:49,473 --> 00:07:52,224
The jury's total came
to forty-three million,

150
00:07:52,291 --> 00:07:57,356
eight hundred and ninety-nine thousand,
seven hundred and twenty-five violations.

151
00:07:57,423 --> 00:08:01,975
Five of those were for something the
law treats as worse than misleading:

152
00:08:02,042 --> 00:08:03,616
unconscionable practices.

153
00:08:03,683 --> 00:08:07,324
That is not forty-three million
victims. It is a counting method,

154
00:08:07,391 --> 00:08:09,843
and Meta's appeal will
attack exactly that.

155
00:08:10,273 --> 00:08:12,458
At five thousand dollars a violation,

156
00:08:12,525 --> 00:08:16,656
the law allowed two hundred and
nineteen point five billion dollars.

157
00:08:16,723 --> 00:08:19,548
At the penalty hearing
on the first of October,

158
00:08:19,615 --> 00:08:23,176
the state's lawyer asked for
thirty-five to forty billion.

159
00:08:23,243 --> 00:08:26,703
Meta asked the court to stop at
three point four five billion.

160
00:08:27,133 --> 00:08:28,556
So the judge had a range,

161
00:08:28,623 --> 00:08:32,906
from about three and a half billion
to two hundred and nineteen billion.

162
00:08:32,973 --> 00:08:37,123
Three weeks later, Judge Francis
Mathew signed a twenty-page judgment.

163
00:08:37,553 --> 00:08:40,353
Judge Mathew wrote that
Facebook's wrongdoing "goes

164
00:08:40,420 --> 00:08:43,343
to the heart of Facebook's
core business practices."

165
00:08:43,773 --> 00:08:47,119
Then the judge cut the penalty
to twenty-five billion dollars.

166
00:08:47,186 --> 00:08:49,333
The law allowed almost nine times more.

167
00:08:49,763 --> 00:08:53,123
Interest runs at fifteen
percent a year until it is paid.

168
00:08:53,190 --> 00:08:54,933
More orders could still come.

169
00:08:55,353 --> 00:08:59,523
And the money goes to the State of
New Mexico. Not to a single user.

170
00:08:59,953 --> 00:09:01,695
Meta said it would appeal,

171
00:09:01,762 --> 00:09:06,143
and its chief legal officer said
he was confident it would win.

172
00:09:06,573 --> 00:09:10,376
Meta's position is simple. It
says it does not sell user data.

173
00:09:10,443 --> 00:09:14,313
And it says the state never proved
that anyone was actually misled.

174
00:09:14,743 --> 00:09:18,468
In its quarterly filing, Meta
set money aside for the penalty,

175
00:09:18,535 --> 00:09:20,003
without saying how much.

176
00:09:20,433 --> 00:09:23,986
So how big is twenty-five
billion dollars for this company?

177
00:09:24,053 --> 00:09:27,023
It is about thirty-nine percent
of one quarter's revenue.

178
00:09:27,453 --> 00:09:30,167
It is about thirty
percent of the eighty-two

179
00:09:30,234 --> 00:09:33,386
billion dollars Meta holds
in cash and securities.

180
00:09:33,453 --> 00:09:36,891
Painful. But not fatal.
In the same quarter,

181
00:09:36,958 --> 00:09:39,759
its revenue grew twenty-six percent,

182
00:09:39,826 --> 00:09:44,923
and three point six four billion
people used its apps every day.

183
00:09:45,353 --> 00:09:48,976
The real pressure in that same
quarter came from somewhere else.

184
00:09:49,043 --> 00:09:52,038
Meta booked a nine point
eight nine billion dollar

185
00:09:52,105 --> 00:09:55,956
charge for a separate settlement
over teenagers' mental health.

186
00:09:56,023 --> 00:09:58,773
Its operating income fell
thirty-seven percent.

187
00:09:59,203 --> 00:10:03,142
It spent almost thirty-five
billion dollars in three months,

188
00:10:03,209 --> 00:10:06,606
mostly on data centres for
artificial intelligence.

189
00:10:06,673 --> 00:10:11,593
So its free cash flow turned negative,
minus two point one two billion dollars.

190
00:10:12,023 --> 00:10:14,852
It plans to sell ninety-seven
and a half billion

191
00:10:14,919 --> 00:10:17,693
dollars of new stock to
pay for the build-out.

192
00:10:18,123 --> 00:10:19,838
On the thirtieth of October,

193
00:10:19,905 --> 00:10:23,716
the ratings agency S and P moved
Meta's outlook to negative.

194
00:10:23,783 --> 00:10:26,533
Meta replied that it
"respectfully" disagreed.

195
00:10:26,963 --> 00:10:32,274
So the company that can absorb a
twenty-five billion dollar penalty is,

196
00:10:32,341 --> 00:10:37,113
at the same time, selling new
shares to pay for its own future.

197
00:10:37,553 --> 00:10:42,153
So here is the lesson. And it goes back
to that promise from twenty twelve.

198
00:10:42,583 --> 00:10:45,135
Meta did not fall when the scandal broke,

199
00:10:45,202 --> 00:10:48,836
because the scandal was the
business working as designed.

200
00:10:48,903 --> 00:10:51,958
The quiz used a permission
Facebook gave every app.

201
00:10:52,025 --> 00:10:55,453
The data was valuable because
Facebook made it valuable.

202
00:10:55,883 --> 00:11:00,273
The fines were records, and the company
paid them out of a growing pile.

203
00:11:00,703 --> 00:11:05,796
What changed in twenty twenty-six was
not the scandal. It was the arithmetic.

204
00:11:05,863 --> 00:11:10,443
One state refused to settle, and
counted the cost statement by statement.

205
00:11:10,873 --> 00:11:14,813
The bill is ordered. It is not paid.

206
00:11:15,243 --> 00:11:16,613
If the appeal fails,

207
00:11:16,680 --> 00:11:19,487
New Mexico will have shown that a broken

208
00:11:19,554 --> 00:11:22,792
promise can be priced
one statement at a time,

209
00:11:22,859 --> 00:11:25,726
at a scale that even Meta has to notice.

210
00:11:25,793 --> 00:11:26,822
If it succeeds,

211
00:11:26,889 --> 00:11:32,811
then in the end the biggest bill in this
story will stay exactly what it is today,

212
00:11:32,878 --> 00:11:36,024
a number on paper that
nobody has ever paid,

213
00:11:36,091 --> 00:11:40,473
because the arithmetic behind
it did not survive the appeal.

214
00:11:40,903 --> 00:11:42,855
So tell us what you think:

215
00:11:42,922 --> 00:11:48,056
should a company pay for every
misleading thing it tells its users,

216
00:11:48,123 --> 00:11:51,376
or only for what someone can prove it did?

217
00:11:51,443 --> 00:11:52,893
Tell us in the comments.

